-- title:
--   Constant term of q-expansions as an R-point of the pole chart
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be an integrally closed commutative domain equipped with an algebra structure over $\mathbb{Q}$ making $\mathbb{Q}$ its fraction field, and let $N$ be a nonzero natural number. Write $F =$ `modularFunctionFieldFull N` for the intermediate field of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ generated by the set of Laurent series $\mathrm{qExpand}_{\mathbb{Q}}\,d\,(j_q)$ for the nonzero divisors $d$ of $N$; assume $F$ is an $R$-algebra in a way compatible with its $\mathbb{Q}$-structure, i.e. $R \to \mathbb{Q} \to F$ is a tower. Let $j =$ `jFull N` be the element $j_q$ of $F$, and let $A_\infty =$ `chartAlgInf R F j`, the $R$-subalgebra of $F$ consisting of those elements that are integral over the $R$-subalgebra generated by $j^{-1}$, that is, the integral closure of $R[1/j]$ in $F$. The assertion is that there exists an $R$-algebra homomorphism $\varphi \colon A_\infty \to R$ such that for every $x \in A_\infty$ the image of $\varphi(x)$ under the structure map $R \to \mathbb{Q}$ equals the coefficient of $q^0$ in the Laurent series underlying $x$; thus $\varphi$ is the constant-term map on $q$-expansions.
--
--   This exhibits the cusp at infinity as an $R$-valued point of the chart at infinity of the two-chart integral model of the modular curve, for any integrally closed base with fraction field $\mathbb{Q}$; it is the statement that the constant term of the $q$-expansion of a function regular at the cusp lies in $R$. It is used in the specialisation [`ModularCurve.IgusaScheme.exists_algHom_int_chartAlgInf_eq_coeff_zero`](thm.html#ModularCurve.IgusaScheme.exists_algHom_int_chartAlgInf_eq_coeff_zero).
